{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When you acquire.</b> I say to you a general principle before I begin to interpret: every law or commandment stands on its own, and if we can find a reason why this law was joined to that one, or this commandment to that one, we shall join them with all our ability, and if we cannot, we shall consider that the deficiency stems from the lack of our knowledge. And there is nothing in the world harder for a person than being under the authority of a person like himself; therefore he began with the law of the slave. And our predecessors said that עבד עברי is an Israelite, and likewise וכי ימכר איש את בתו לאמה (Exodus 21:7). Only וכי יכה איש את שן עבדו (below v. 26) is a Canaanite [slave], and likewise וכי יכה איש את עבדו (ibid. v. 20), and likewise אם עבד יגח השור (ibid. v. 32). And behold, they said that ונמכר בגנבתו (below v. 22:2) is an Israelite. And the disputants said that עבד עברי is not an Israelite but rather from the family of Abraham, and likewise the daughter, and all the slaves mentioned thereafter are one category for all. And their proof is the matter of the wife and her children, for they said that the family depends on the father — and furthermore, ועבדו לעולם (v. 6), for they say [it means] all his days, and it was difficult for them that an Israelite should be bored through [the ear]. And now let us examine the words according to their plain meaning. If עבד עברי is not an Israelite, why did Scripture say, כי ימכר לך אחיך העברי (Deuteronomy 15:12)? And do we not know that the meaning of לא תשנא את אחיך בלבבך (Leviticus 19:17) is an Israelite, and likewise בין איש ובין אחיו ובין גרו (Deuteronomy 1:16), and many like these? And behold, there is no doubt that the interpretation of אברם העברי is that he is of the descendants of Eber, about whom it is written אבי כל בני עבר (Genesis 10:21), the opposite of וחם הוא אבי כנען (ibid. 9:18). Or he was called עברי because he came from across the river, even though it should have been עברי נהרי. And we consider that Scripture took a short path, because we found ואקח את אביכם את אברהם מעבר הנהר (Joshua 24:3). And behold, the Hebrews too were so called, who are Israelites, for we saw that Moses said to Pharaoh כה אמר ה' אלהי ישראל (above 5:1), and when Pharaoh asked who is this YHWH, then they answered אלהי העברים (ibid. v. 3). And behold, אלהי העברים is like אלהי ישראל. And behold, if Israelites were called עברים because they are descended from Eber, the children of Ishmael and the children of Keturah and the children of Esau would share this with us. And we have not found [Scripture] calling אחיך except Edom alone, who was in one womb with Jacob. Also you will not find in Scripture that it mentions Esau as our brother in commandments, only in the matter of agency and the like: לא תתעב אדומי כי אחיך הוא (Deuteronomy 23:8) — after it mentioned אדומי, and likewise על רדפו בחרב אחיו (Amos 1:11). Behold, in all of Scripture we have not found the word עברי except for the children of Jacob. And it is written: ושם אתנו נער עברי (Genesis 41:12); לאכול את העברים לחם (ibid. 43:32); מכה איש עברי (above 2:11); שני אנשים עברים נצים (ibid.). And Jonah said עברי אנכי (Jonah 1:9), for they asked him אי מזה עם אתה (ibid. v. 8), and if the word had encompassed many peoples, he still would not have answered which people he was. And it is written פן תעבדון לעברים (1 Samuel 4:9) and העברים היו לפלשתים (ibid. 14:21). And in Jeremiah it is written לשלח איש את עבדו וגו' העברי והעבריה (Jeremiah 34:9), and at the end he explained לבלתי עבוד בם ביהודי אחיהו איש (ibid.), and it is known that an Edomite is not called a יהודי. And the disputants said: behold, it is written ורבים מעמי הארץ מתיהדים (Esther 8:17), and they are not Israelites. And this is blindness, for according to their own opinion, if the meaning is that they traced their lineage to Judah or that they returned to the Torah of Judah, it should have said לבלתי עבד בם במתיהד אחיהו, and in this manner it should have been עברי — מתעבר. And [as for] the one who says this: מתעבר על ריב לא לו (Proverbs 26:17). And behold, it has been established that the words of our predecessors are correct, for upon them we rely in all the commandments as they received them from their fathers. And furthermore, how could it enter a person's mind that the Ishmaelite and the Edomite would serve six years, and the Israelite until the Jubilee year?" "<b>Six years he shall serve.</b> And no more, like שש שנים תזרע שדך (Leviticus 25:3). And at the beginning of the seventh year from his sale he goes out free, whichever year it may be. And do not wonder about מקץ שבע שנים written in Jeremiah (Jeremiah 34:14), for every thing has two ends, and behold, sometimes קץ is found meaning the beginning, and sometimes the end. And this slave is the one about whom Scripture writes ונמכר בגנבתו (below 22:2), whom the court sold. But וכי ימוך אחיך ונמכר לך (Leviticus 25:39), or to a foreigner — he sold himself, according to the years remaining until the Jubilee, for in the Jubilee he goes out. And it is a tradition in Israel that the male is sold for his theft but not the female; in the portion after this I shall complete the matter. Rabbi Marinus said: the yod in לחפשי is added, for it is like למשעי (Ezekiel 16:4), אכזרי (Proverbs 11:17). And R. Judah the Grammarian said that the lamed is added, for it is like וכי תשלחנו חפשי (Deuteronomy 15:13), and the yod is as if for attribution, in the manner of the ancient language (Ezekiel 10:19), and the lamed is like the lamed in והשלישי לאבשלום בן מעכה (1 Chronicles 3:2). And in my opinion, two servitors came and one would have sufficed, like בתוך האהלי (Joshua 7:21), and it is an adjective form of the noun." "And the word חנם is from the root of חנונו אותם (Judges 21:22), in the sense of gift. And the reason for the mem we do not know, like the mem of ריקם (Genesis 31:42)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "The phrase 'two servitors came' (שני משרתים באו) is opaque in the original; translated hyper-literally as the context concerns whether the yod and lamed are both superfluous additions to חפשי."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:36:59.626161117Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}